WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
WHAT HAPPENED TO SKYPE:
In May of 2011, Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:09.720
announced that they would be buying Skype for&nbsp;
$8.5 billion in cash. From the very beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:14.820
it seemed like Microsoft had overpaid for the&nbsp;
video conferencing platform. Just two years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:22.140
before this, Skype had been sold for a third of&nbsp;
this price coming in at $2.75 billion. There was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:28.500
no way Skype’s value had tripled within these&nbsp;
24 months, especially given the 2008 financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:34.200
recession. And Microsoft very much knew this,&nbsp;
but if they wanted to get Skype, this was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:39.360
price they had to pay because two other giants,&nbsp;
Google and Facebook, were also interested in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:45.180
acquiring Skype. It appears that Microsoft had&nbsp;
won the bidding wars, but now, it was time for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:50.340
the actual hard stuff. They needed to ensure&nbsp;
that this overpriced purchase would actually be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:56.880
worth it over the long term. Unfortunately, by all&nbsp;
accounts, they would miserably fail at doing this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:03.240
Public distaste for Skype simply grew year after&nbsp;
year and people were simply waiting for a viable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:12.720
alternative to pop up. For gamers, this turned out&nbsp;
to be Discord. For businesses and institutions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:18.240
this turned out to be Zoom. Things actually&nbsp;
got so bad that Microsoft themselves shutdown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:23.100
Skype for business in the middle of 2021.&nbsp;
Hearing all this, you would think that Skype&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:28.200
was a massive failure, and technically&nbsp;
Skype itself was indeed a failure. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:35.580
what if I told you that Skype as an investment&nbsp;
was actually a massive win for Microsoft. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:40.980
just as Skype started hitting its breaking point&nbsp;
in the late 2010s, Microsoft went out and iterated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:47.040
on Skype themselves and created a new solution:&nbsp;
Microsoft Teams. If you’re a tech worker, you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:53.400
probably extremely familiar with Microsoft Teams,&nbsp;
and this sound probably gives you nightmares. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:58.740
you’re not familiar with Teams, this is basically&nbsp;
the business equivalent of this sound. Anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:04.620
according to Microsoft themselves, Teams is&nbsp;
their fastest growing business app of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:10.620
time. And let’s not forget, Microsoft was&nbsp;
also the creators of Office and Outlook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:17.820
so this is truly an achievement. Between March and&nbsp;
June of 2020, Teams actually grew faster than Zoom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:26.460
growing a whopping 894%. And today, Teams has&nbsp;
a total of 270 million users, which is not that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:32.400
far behind Zoom’s 300 million active users. Not&nbsp;
to mention, Teams users are way more profitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:38.160
given that they're more likely to be business&nbsp;
users. In 2020 for example, Zoom pulled in just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:44.940
over $4 billion while Teams actually pulled in&nbsp;
nearly $7 billion. Yet, all the media ever talks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:51.600
about is Zoom. So, heres’ the rise and fall&nbsp;
of Skype and its insane resurrection as Teams.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.280
THE RISE OF SKYPE:&nbsp;

00:02:55.380 --> 00:03:01.200
Taking a look back, the story of Skype dates&nbsp;
back to 2003 to two Scandinavian men named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.865
Niklas Zennstrom and Janus Friis. The idea behind&nbsp;
Skype wasn't to crack into the business world or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.865 --> 00:03:05.040
dominate video conferencing. The idea was actually&nbsp;
much simpler. The idea was to substantially reduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.260
the cost of long distance phone calls. If you have&nbsp;
family in other countries, you’re probably well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:16.200
aware of just how expensive this is, and this&nbsp;
is still the case to this day. But, nowadays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:21.000
you’re first instinct wouldn’t be to hope on&nbsp;
Skype. It would probably be to use Facetime or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:27.360
WhatsApp. Back in the early 2000s though, these&nbsp;
options didn’t even exist. So, you either had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:33.540
to pay AT&amp;T an arm and a leg or you had to put up&nbsp;
with MSN messenger. Skype’s founders were acutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:39.240
aware of this situation and they felt that the&nbsp;
industry was just asking for disruption. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:44.160
like any good entrepreneurs, they got to work.&nbsp;
They would partner with some Estonian developers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.620
and they would create a minimal viable product by&nbsp;
the end of the year. And they would call it Skype&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:55.500
which stood for “Sky peer to peer”. Skype&nbsp;
was a massive hit almost instantaneously.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:00.300
There was clearly a bunch of cooped up demand&nbsp;
that was just waiting for a better solution, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:05.700
Skype was exactly that. The founders built on this&nbsp;
success by expanding into traditional phone calls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:11.160
as well with the launch of SkypeOut. SkypeOut&nbsp;
allowed users to directly call a landline or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:17.100
mobile phone from their computers. This probably&nbsp;
doesn’t sound like a big deal today, but back in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:24.000
2005, this was insane because not everyone even&nbsp;
had computers. This meant that you could leverage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.940
Skype even if only one person had access to a&nbsp;
computer, and the best part was that it costed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:36.720
a mere 2 cents per minute. For perspective, if&nbsp;
you don’t have an international plan, AT&amp;T will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:44.880
shamelessly charge you $2.71 per minute and that’s&nbsp;
in 2022. Who knows how much AT&amp;T charged in 2005.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:51.660
Anyway, the cost and convenience of Skype made it&nbsp;
a no brainer. Within 18 months of launching, Skype&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:56.880
would cross 23 million registered users and within&nbsp;
2 years, they would cross 100 million downloads.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.400
Skype was still very much just a startup, but&nbsp;
bigger corporations were already interested in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:07.740
acquiring the business. And we weren’t talking&nbsp;
about tens of millions or even hundreds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:14.460
millions, we were talking about billions, $2.6&nbsp;
billion from eBay to be exact. Skype had gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:22.800
from 0 to $2.6 billion in matter of 2.5 years. Not&nbsp;
a bad days work, but Skype was still just getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:29.160
started. At the end of 2005, Skype would launch&nbsp;
video calling and in mid 2006, they would launch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:35.640
support for massive calls with up to 100 people.&nbsp;
By the end of 2007, Skype would reach 220 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:42.120
registered users and by all accounts, they were&nbsp;
crushing it. But, despite all this growth, eBay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:47.460
was finding it more and more difficult to keep up&nbsp;
with Skype. There was virtually no synergy between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:52.380
the two companies, so it was basically like&nbsp;
running two different businesses. Soon enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:57.420
eBay just wanted out, so they would sell Skype&nbsp;
to the first buyer they could find for a terrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:03.360
valuation of $2.75 billion. These buyers would&nbsp;
simply turn around and flip Skype to Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:10.080
a few years later for $8.6 billion in cold hard&nbsp;
cash, and that brings us into the Microsoft era.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:14.220
THE DEATH OF SKYPE:&nbsp;

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:19.980
Microsoft bought the top. That’s simply the truth&nbsp;
and there’s no way around it. To make things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:26.340
worse, not only did they buy the top, but they&nbsp;
overpaid at the top due to the bidding war. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.340 --> 00:06:32.220
wait a minute, hold up what happened? Wasn’t Skype&nbsp;
growing at an insane pace with no end in sight?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:38.160
Well, unfortunately, the reality was that there&nbsp;
was an end in sight and reasoning was no obvious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:43.860
The number one selling factor of Skype was its&nbsp;
functionality. People didn’t use it because it had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:50.220
a cool UI or great integration and synergy or any&nbsp;
of that. People used it simply because it solved a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:55.020
problem. But, as we moved into the 2010s, there&nbsp;
were several services that could solve the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.020 --> 00:07:00.540
problem, but these other services also came with&nbsp;
a bunch of extra bells and whistles. WhatsApp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:05.940
for example, actually offered an enjoyable&nbsp;
mobile experience unlike Skype. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:11.220
Google Hangouts and Apple Facetime were able to&nbsp;
tap into the massive networks of Gmail and iPhone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:17.640
users. And for most people, switching to these&nbsp;
services was a no brainer. Why settle for just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:22.800
functionality when you could opt for functionality&nbsp;
and a great user experience. This isn’t to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:28.080
that Skype stopped growing per se. In fact,&nbsp;
in terms of pure numbers, they continued their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:34.140
exponential growth into the billions of users. The&nbsp;
problem though was that these weren’t happy users.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:39.840
These were users that were becoming increasingly&nbsp;
frustrated, and the only reason they used Skype&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:42.960
was because everyone around them was using&nbsp;
it. It was the classic case of, it was popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:48.480
because it was already popular. Likely the&nbsp;
two biggest complaints about skype were that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:54.240
the UI sucked and the service itself was not as&nbsp;
reliable as competitors. The first point is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:59.580
that surprising. I mean, we’re talking about&nbsp;
Microsoft here. Despite their enormous size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:04.860
they’ve never been able to get mobile right.&nbsp;
I mean just think about the Windows Phone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:10.560
which lost them $8 billion. The problem was&nbsp;
that Microsoft was always trying to import&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:15.840
a computer basd experience onto mobile devices&nbsp;
which simply didn’t translate well. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:20.400
their competitors were reimagining how every&nbsp;
task could be done on a phone from scratch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:25.620
and the competitors destroyed Microsoft&nbsp;
everytime. As for the reliability problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:31.980
this wasn’t exactly Microsoft’s fault, but they&nbsp;
weren’t able to fix it either. The reason that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:37.476
Skype was so unreliable was because it was a&nbsp;
peer to peer service. The reliability of a peer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.476 --> 00:08:42.480
to peer connection was simply uncomparable to a&nbsp;
server based connection backed by a $13 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:49.200
data center. Microsoft tried to address this by&nbsp;
moving Skype to the cloud, but this move was kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:55.140
of always botched. Not to mention, this move&nbsp;
also kind of backfired as it made people think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.140 --> 00:09:00.120
that Microsoft was trying to track their data. And&nbsp;
if you were gonna let Microsoft collect your data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:04.560
you might as well let Google and Facebook&nbsp;
collect your data. At least, you’ll have a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:09.900
UI there. And if you want some quantitative data&nbsp;
regarding just how much people hated Skype, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:17.700
in June of 2017, the average app store rating&nbsp;
for Skype drop from 3.5 stars to 1.5 stars. Yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:25.560
it was that bad, but at the same time, no one&nbsp;
cared enough to switch either until the pandemic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:30.360
People figured that if they were gonna use video&nbsp;
conferencing on a regular basis, they had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:36.120
find a better solution and the answer was Zoom.&nbsp;
And this pretty much sealed the fate of Skype.

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:40.260
RESURRECTION AS TEAMS:&nbsp;

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:45.720
During all of this, Microsoft was by no&nbsp;
means oblivious to what was happening. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:51.660
were literally waiting to get destroyed in the&nbsp;
consumer market. But, at the same time, Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:58.080
oddly didn’t mind. You see, the entire company&nbsp;
was moving away from consumer products. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:03.840
they’ve literally made Windows and Office free for&nbsp;
consumers. The reason for this is that Microsoft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:10.080
is no longer a consumer business. The majority of&nbsp;
their revenue actually comes from b2b revenue for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:16.860
things like cloud infrastructure and licensing.&nbsp;
With that being said, Skype no longer fit inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:22.860
their wheelhouse. Something that did fit though&nbsp;
was business video conferencing. Microsoft already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:27.900
had strong relationships with virtually every&nbsp;
company in the world given that basically every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:33.360
company has to licenses Windows, Office, and&nbsp;
Outlook. So, why not sell them another solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:38.520
that seamlessly integrated with these products.&nbsp;
And this led to the launch of Skype for Business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:45.060
in 2015. Microsoft quickly realized though that&nbsp;
the Skype branding wasn’t doing them any favors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:50.760
so they would embark on a complete rebranding&nbsp;
which led to the launch of Microsoft Teams in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:56.700
2017. It wasn’t all sunshines and rainbows though&nbsp;
as most businesses already had established video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.700 --> 00:11:03.420
conferencing systems from Cisco. Cisco Webex was&nbsp;
nothing to brag about, but it got the job done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:10.020
and companies didn’t see a reason to switch. As&nbsp;
a result, adoption of Teams was lackluster to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:16.020
the least. By the end of 2017, Teams only had&nbsp;
2 million users, and even by the end of 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:23.400
Teams only had 8 million users. 2019 was a much&nbsp;
better year for Teams as they finally got into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:29.580
tens of millions of users, but the breakthrough&nbsp;
obviously didn’t come till 2020. As we all know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:35.280
2020 forced everyone to reevaluate their video&nbsp;
conferencing solutions, and the choice was pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:41.580
clear. WebEx was an obvious no. If you’re not&nbsp;
familiar with WebEx, it’s basically the business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:48.240
equivalent of Skype. This left companies with&nbsp;
two options: Zoom or Teams. Both products offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:53.460
leading conferencing solutions, but Teams had a&nbsp;
slight leg up on Zoom which was integration with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.460 --> 00:11:58.140
Microsoft’s other products. For companies&nbsp;
that already relied on Microsoft products,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.140 --> 00:12:03.840
this synergy was simply too good to pass on,&nbsp;
and I think you can guess what happened next.

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:07.620
THE DOMINATION OF TEAMS:&nbsp;

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:13.680
Today, Teams doesn’t receive all that much&nbsp;
attention just like Microsoft themselves. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:19.260
don’t let this fool you, Teams is the real&nbsp;
king of video conferencing. For most people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:25.380
using Zoom was an unfortunate temporary reality,&nbsp;
and they ditched Zoom as soon as they could. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:31.620
is very much reflected in Zoom’s stock graph.&nbsp;
For most companies, however, using Teams is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:36.600
permanent solution. And given that companies are&nbsp;
pretty hesitant on switching services, they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:43.080
probably locked in for several years if not&nbsp;
decades. So, Microsoft’s $8.5 billion acquisition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:50.400
has turned into $6.8 billion in revenue every&nbsp;
single year. And that was as of 2020 when Teams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:57.900
had 75 million users. Since then, their user count&nbsp;
has grown nearly 4 fold to 270 million. So Teams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:05.400
revenue today is definitely above $10 billion and&nbsp;
probably closer $20 billion. And if you’re still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:11.580
not convinced about Team’s success, just listen&nbsp;
to this. Last quarter, Microsoft made $5.3 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:17.820
from Windows, their most well known product. If&nbsp;
we annualize this, we get about $21.2 billion per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:24.720
year. In other words, Team’s revenue is literally&nbsp;
comparable to that of Windows, and it’s probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:31.080
just a matter of time until Teams overtakes&nbsp;
Windows altogether. Also, the sector that Teams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:39.120
is part of is already at $11.5 billion per quarter&nbsp;
which is twice of Windows, so who knows, maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:45.240
Teams has already overtaken Windows. And that’s&nbsp;
why Skype was likely Microsoft’s best acquisition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:52.200
of all time. Skype itself didn’t work out all&nbsp;
that well, but it gave rise to the behemoth that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:57.840
is Teams. Did you hate Skype? Comment that down&nbsp;
below. Also, drop a like if you would like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

